# PRACTICAL 01 – Sorting Algorithms

## Aim
To implement different sorting algorithms in Python and analyze their time complexity and execution time.

## Algorithms Implemented

### 1. Bubble Sort
Repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.

- Best Case: O(n)*
- Average Case: O(n²)
- Worst Case: O(n²)
- Space: O(1)

### 2. Selection Sort
Finds the smallest element from the unsorted part and places it at the correct position.

- Best Case: O(n²)
- Average Case: O(n²)
- Worst Case: O(n²)
- Space: O(1)
- Comparisons: `n(n-1)/2`

### 3. Insertion Sort
Builds the sorted list one element at a time by inserting each element into its correct position.

- Best Case: O(n)
- Average Case: O(n²)
- Worst Case: O(n²)
- Space: O(1)

### 4. Quick Sort
Uses a pivot to partition the array into smaller and larger elements and recursively sorts them.

- Best Case: O(n log n)
- Average Case: O(n log n)
- Worst Case: O(n²)
- Space: O(log n) average recursion stack

### 5. Merge Sort
Divides the list into smaller parts, sorts them recursively, and merges the sorted parts.

- Best Case: O(n log n)
- Average Case: O(n log n)
- Worst Case: O(n log n)
- Space: O(n)

## Sample Outputs

### Bubble Sort
```text
Original list: [5, 7, 2, 1, 8, 4, 6]
Sorted list: [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
Time Complexity: O(n²)
